# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Line endings
#
# The author develops on Windows, where `core.autocrlf=true` is the Git for
# Windows default: it converts LF to CRLF on checkout and back to LF on commit.
# That default is a setting, not a guarantee — a contributor (or the same author
# on a differently-configured box) with `core.autocrlf=false` and an editor that
# saves CRLF would commit CRLF into these files, and nothing in the tree would
# object.
#
# The failure that causes is remote from its cause. CI runs
# `bash scripts/install-linux-deps.sh`; with CRLF in the file, bash reads the
# trailing \r as part of every command and dies with
#
#     scripts/install-linux-deps.sh: line 2: $'\r': command not found
#
# which names no line ending and points at the wrong line. `text eol=lf` pins
# the working-tree form for anything a POSIX shell or a shebang has to read,
# regardless of who cloned it or how their Git is configured.
*.sh   text eol=lf
*.py   text eol=lf

# CMake, YAML and JSON are read by tools that do not care about CRLF, but they
# are also the files most often diffed across platforms, and a whole-file diff
# caused by a line-ending flip hides the one line that actually changed.
*.cmake        text eol=lf
CMakeLists.txt text eol=lf
*.yml          text eol=lf
*.json         text eol=lf

# Our own sources. Not a correctness matter — MSVC, GCC and Clang all read CRLF
# — but the same "keep diffs honest" reason as above.
*.h    text eol=lf
*.cpp  text eol=lf
*.md   text eol=lf
*.html text eol=lf

# Event-log fixtures, and this one IS a correctness matter. A replay fixture is
# committed so the engine can be required to reproduce it byte for byte; if Git
# handed a Windows checkout the same file with CRLF endings, "byte for byte"
# would mean two different things on two platforms. The reader tolerates CRLF,
# deliberately, so a log that has been through an editor still opens — but the
# committed fixtures are LF everywhere, which is also what the writer emits
# (EventLog opens its file in binary mode for exactly this reason).
*.wfglog text eol=lf

# Show documents, and the canonical fixtures in particular. The writer emits LF
# on every platform and a test compares its output with the committed file byte
# for byte; letting Git hand a Windows checkout CRLF would make "byte for byte"
# mean two different things on two machines.
*.xml    text eol=lf

# The generated grammar and the bundle manifest, and both are the same
# correctness matter as the .xml rule above rather than a tidiness one.
#
# `wfg schema --check` compares the committed .rng with what the parameter table
# generates, as BYTES - it reads the file with loadFileAsData precisely so that
# no reader can normalise a line ending out of existence and report a match
# between a CRLF file and an LF one. Without this rule a Windows checkout would
# hand it CRLF and the gate would fail on the machine that had just written the
# file correctly. The manifest is compared the same way, by the bundle
# round-trip test.
#
# Caught by the warning Git prints on `git add`, which is worth reading rather
# than scrolling past: it says exactly which files have no rule.
*.rng    text eol=lf
*.wfg    text eol=lf

# The parameter table, for the same reason and one more: it is generated FROM
# and read BY the build, and a spreadsheet round trip on Windows would otherwise
# rewrite every line of the diff to say nothing.
*.csv    text eol=lf

# The sanitizer suppression list, and this is a correctness matter rather than a
# tidiness one. RTSAN_OPTIONS=suppressions=... is read by the Clang runtime on
# Linux, which matches each line as a pattern; a trailing  would be part of the
# symbol being matched and every suppression would silently stop suppressing -
# leaving a CI job that reports thousands of known Tracktion locks and looks like
# a real-time failure that had just appeared.
*.txt  text eol=lf

# PowerShell too. It is Windows-only by definition and CRLF would be the more
# conventional choice there, but bootstrap.ps1 is LF on disk today, PowerShell
# 5.1 and 7 both read LF without complaint, and one rule for the whole tree beats
# an exception nobody remembers the reason for.
*.ps1  text eol=lf

# Git's own metadata files and the licence. Git parses .gitattributes and
# .gitmodules with either line ending, so this is not a correctness matter - it
# is the last four tracked files that had no rule, and leaving them out meant
# `git add` kept printing a conversion warning that everyone would learn to
# scroll past. A warning nobody reads is worse than no warning, because the next
# real one looks the same.
.gitattributes text eol=lf
.gitignore     text eol=lf
.gitmodules    text eol=lf
LICENSE        text eol=lf

# Nothing here reaches ThirdParty/: those are two gitlinks, and Git does not
# apply a superproject's attributes across a submodule boundary. JUCE and
# Tracktion Engine carry their own.
